WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.766 --> 00:00:10.934
In 1893, a young Indian man left his home to earn a living in South Africa.

2
00:00:10.935 --> 00:00:18.966
Soon after he arrived, he needed to travel to the capital, so his employer purchased a first-class train ticket for him.

3
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:30.406
Mohandas Gandhi took his seat in the first-class compartment, but at one of the stations along the way, a white passenger boarded the train and refused to share the compartment with an Indian.

4
00:00:30.407 --> 00:00:35.388
The white man called the conductor, who told Gandhi to go to the luggage car.

5
00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:39.381
In his autobiography, Gandhi describes what happened:

6
00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:43.277
"But, I have a first-class ticket," Gandhi protested.

7
00:00:43.278 --> 00:00:48.335
"That doesn't matter," the conductor replied. "You must go to the luggage compartment."

8
00:00:48.336 --> 00:00:56.264
"I tell you, I was permitted at Durban to travel in this compartment," Gandhi objected, "and I insist on staying in it."

9
00:00:56.265 --> 00:01:03.924
When the conductor realized that Gandhi would not move, he called a police officer, and together, they threw him off the train.

10
00:01:03.925 --> 00:01:08.653
This incident was the spark that began Gandhi's political activism.

11
00:01:08.654 --> 00:01:19.322
Humiliated and left by the side of the railroad tracks, Gandhi would one day lead India to independence and inspire movements for civil rights and freedom across the world.

12
00:01:19.323 --> 00:01:23.128
Gandhi was born on October 2, 1869.

13
00:01:23.129 --> 00:01:28.760
His parents were faithful Hindus and part of a well-respected caste of traders.

14
00:01:28.761 --> 00:01:33.807
The caste system divided India's population into different social classes.

15
00:01:33.808 --> 00:01:40.734
Some castes were held in high esteem; some were considered so low that their members were called "untouchables."

16
00:01:40.735 --> 00:01:44.461
As a young man, Gandhi went to study law in London.

17
00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:52.604
Members of his caste, however, believed that by going to London, Gandhi was abandoning his faith, and they expelled him from the caste.

18
00:01:52.605 --> 00:01:59.833
While studying, Gandhi held true to his Hindu beliefs, but when he returned home, people avoided him anyway.

19
00:01:59.834 --> 00:02:06.284
He found he could not earn a living as a lawyer in India, so he decided to try his luck in South Africa.

20
00:02:06.285 --> 00:02:14.045
South Africa had a large Indian community, and Gandhi was able to find work in the city of Durban in Natal province.

21
00:02:14.046 --> 00:02:19.257
Unfortunately, discrimination against Indians was common in South Africa.

22
00:02:19.258 --> 00:02:25.740
Indians could not leave their homes after 9:00 p.m., and businesses commonly refused to serve Indians.

23
00:02:25.741 --> 00:02:32.018
During Gandhi's time there, the government prepared legislation that would deny Indians the right to vote.

24
00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:37.517
Later, the government even decided to declare all Hindu marriages invalid.

25
00:02:37.518 --> 00:02:44.515
In 1894, Gandhi founded the Natal Indian Congress in an effort to combat discrimination.

26
00:02:44.516 --> 00:02:54.463
For the next 12 years, Gandhi was politically active in South Africa, but he found that his attempts to protect Indians from prejudice largely failed.

27
00:02:54.464 --> 00:02:57.563
Letters, petitions, and protests did not help.

28
00:02:57.564 --> 00:03:02.273
Gandhi decided that the strategy behind the Indians' protests must change.

29
00:03:02.274 --> 00:03:09.105
He asked his protesters to embrace the idea of ahimsa─a Hindu word meaning "nonviolence."

30
00:03:09.106 --> 00:03:16.716
Gandhi realized that if Indians engaged in violent protest, the protest itself would be a reason for more oppression.

31
00:03:16.717 --> 00:03:22.699
Gandhi called his new method of protest satyagraha, or "devotion to truth."

32
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:29.753
When faced with an unjust law, Gandhi and his followers would openly break the law, but not resist arrest.

33
00:03:29.754 --> 00:03:39.017
In 1906, when the government required all Indians to carry identity cards, Gandhi led his people in a satyagraha protest.

34
00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:46.565
South Africa's Indian citizens peacefully went on strike, refused to register, and burned their identity cards.

35
00:03:46.566 --> 00:03:49.569
Thousands were imprisoned, including Gandhi.

36
00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.244
Others were beaten, and some were shot.

37
00:03:52.245 --> 00:04:01.796
After seven years of brutally mistreating a population determined to be peaceful, the government faced a public outcry and was forced to compromise.

38
00:04:01.797 --> 00:04:06.396
The South African government had been shamed into doing the right thing.

39
00:04:06.397 --> 00:04:13.895
When Gandhi returned to India in 1914, he arrived with the reputation of an influential political figure.

40
00:04:13.896 --> 00:04:19.822
He had also built a reputation as a spiritual leader who was deeply committed to peace.

41
00:04:19.823 --> 00:04:31.264
During his time in South Africa, whenever protesters fought back or resisted violently, Gandhi went on a hunger strike until his followers returned to the values of ahimsa.

42
00:04:31.265 --> 00:04:36.094
In India, he continued his fight against oppression and unjust rule.

43
00:04:36.095 --> 00:04:44.205
At the time, India was ruled by the British. Many landless farmers worked for British landlords who paid them very little.

44
00:04:44.206 --> 00:04:50.752
They forced farmers to grow cash crops for export when the farmers themselves were desperately hungry.

45
00:04:50.753 --> 00:04:56.132
When Gandhi organized strikes against foreign landowners, he was quickly arrested.

46
00:04:56.133 --> 00:05:00.294
Thousands of his supporters protested, and the government yielded.

47
00:05:00.295 --> 00:05:07.333
They released Gandhi, and farmers were given more control over the land they farmed so that they could feed their families.

48
00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:14.166
After this, many Indians began to refer to Gandhi as Mahatma, which means "great soul."

49
00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:20.839
Gandhi believed that the lives of Indians would not truly improve unless India gained independence.

50
00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:31.875
The political party most active in India's independence movement was the Congress Party, and although Gandhi never officially joined it, he became its unofficial spiritual leader.

51
00:05:31.876 --> 00:05:38.644
Over the next years, Gandhi led nonviolent protests designed to make British rule over India difficult.

52
00:05:38.645 --> 00:05:44.046
One of Gandhi's most famous protests was the Salt March of 1930.

53
00:05:44.047 --> 00:05:52.762
Anyone in India who made salt was required to pay a tax, and Gandhi considered these "salt laws" to be a symbol of the injustice of British rule.

54
00:05:52.763 --> 00:06:01.581
Salt is a necessity of life and something that Indians could easily make or collect for free, but the salt laws forced them to pay for it.

55
00:06:01.582 --> 00:06:06.625
To protest this law, Gandhi began a march to the coastal town of Dandi.

56
00:06:06.626 --> 00:06:13.069
The 400─kilometer walk took him 28 days, and thousands joined him on his way to the sea.

57
00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:18.838
When they arrived, Gandhi walked to the edge of the ocean and made salt─an illegal act.

58
00:06:18.839 --> 00:06:27.052
The crowd followed his example of peaceful lawbreaking, and as the salt protest spread, over 60,000 were arrested.

59
00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:32.669
If the British wanted to rule India, they would have to imprison tens of thousands of people.

60
00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:41.597
When Britain entered World War II in 1939, it unilaterally included India in the war without consulting India's leaders.

61
00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:49.706
Gandhi, however, believed his country could not help the British fight for their freedom when Britain was denying freedom to the Indian people.

62
00:06:49.707 --> 00:06:56.704
Unless India was granted immediate independence, the most Indians could offer was nonviolent moral support.

63
00:06:56.705 --> 00:07:04.863
As the war progressed, Gandhi increased his demands for independence, drafting a resolution calling for the British to "quit India."

64
00:07:04.864 --> 00:07:07.689
The Quit India movement became massive.

65
00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:17.732
Unfortunately, sometimes ahimsa was forgotten in the excitement; both sides committed violent acts during the years of this struggle, and these troubled Gandhi deeply.

66
00:07:17.733 --> 00:07:24.336
Thousands died and hundreds of thousands were arrested, including Gandhi and the leaders of the Congress Party.

67
00:07:24.337 --> 00:07:31.509
The British held Gandhi for two years to separate him from his followers, but this strategy was not effective.

68
00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:37.509
Gandhi had become a symbol of Indian independence, and the protests only continued to grow.

69
00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:43.054
At the end of the war, the British announced that India would become an independent nation.

70
00:07:43.055 --> 00:07:50.092
Gandhi called off the struggle, and the Congress leadership and over 100,000 political prisoners were released.

71
00:07:50.093 --> 00:07:56.091
India became independent from British rule on August 15, 1947.

72
00:07:56.092 --> 00:08:04.878
India was now free, but religious and ethnic differences led to the partition of the country, creating the state of Pakistan.

73
00:08:04.879 --> 00:08:09.757
Despite the partition, Gandhi worked tirelessly to promote peace.

74
00:08:09.758 --> 00:08:16.477
Sadly, on January 30, 1948, Gandhi was murdered by a religious extremist.

75
00:08:16.478 --> 00:08:26.289
Gandhi's life and teachings have inspired leaders of freedom struggles around the world, including the civil rights movements in the United States and South Africa.

76
00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:37.737
Albert Einstein also believed that we should strive to follow in his footsteps: we should not use violence in fighting for our cause, and refuse to participate in anything we believe is evil.

77
00:08:37.738 --> 00:08:44.993
After Gandhi's death he said, "Generations to come will scarcely believe that such a one as this walked the earth."

78
00:08:44.994 --> 00:08:48.217
Mahatma Gandhi truly was a great soul.
